{"id": "z2106", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 34, "onset_chunk": 33, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So the kittens were raised in a way that they could only see through one eye at a time, and each day their vision was restricted to one eye. As adults, these cats would have trouble with binoc ular depth perception because they never developed the ability to use both eyes together for judging distance s and spatial relationships. The key here is that they couldn' t develop the normal inter ocul ar coordination needed for depth perception. While they might still be able to distinguish left from right or maintain some perceptual constancy, the most significant deficit would be their inability to use binocular cues for depth. So looking at the options, option D says they were unable to use bi nocular cues for depth perception, which directly addresses the core problem caused by this experimental setup. Options A, B, and C focus on specific aspects like mono cular cues or later ality, but the main limitation stems from the lack of binocular experience. Therefore, the best answer is D. They were unable to use binocular cu es for depth perception.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 49, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z2114", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 8, "onset_chunk": 7, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Well, let' s think about this. I know that Independence Day is always on the 4th of July, so that's fixed. New Year' s Day happens every year on January 1st, so that' s also fixed. Christ mas is always December 25th, so that's set in stone. But Thanksgiving? That one changes each year because it' s celebrated on the fourth Thursday of November. So while the others have specific dates, Thanksgiving shifts around depending on the calendar. The answer is C. Thanksgiving.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 26, "carrier_ended": null}
